course: dictionary
lesson: 4951
title: Improvise: making do on the spot
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To improvise means to do something without a plan, using what you have to hand. If the rain starts on a camping trip, you might improvise a shelter by tying a sheet between two trees.
explain: Musicians and actors also improvise. «To improvise means to make up music as you play, within the song's chords and rhythm; good improvisers have practised for years.» So improvising is not random. It follows rules, and it takes practice.
explain: «When an actor forgets a line, making something up to keep the scene going is called improvising, and audiences usually forgive it.» The clues are forgets, making something up and keep the scene going. Together they tell you that improvising means inventing something quickly so that things carry on.
fact: Improvise means to make or do something without planning it first, using what is available.
fact: In music or drama, to improvise means to make up a tune, words or acting on the spot, with no script.
fact: «Improvised» is the past form: «She improvised a bandage from her scarf.»
fact: Good improvisers have usually practised for years, so they can make things up well.
fact: A quick, clever character in a story is often one who can improvise when a plan goes wrong.
exercise: «She improvised a bandage from her scarf when she cut her hand.» What does improvised mean here? | *Made something quickly from what was to hand | Sewed a new scarf carefully over several days | Bought a bandage from the shop | Lost the scarf on the way home
exercise: In a play, an actor forgets a line. What does improvising mean? | *Making something up to keep the scene going | Stopping the play and going home | Reading the line from a script
exercise: The jazz pianist played a tune that nobody had written down. What was she doing? | Practising a scale | *Improvising | Packing up her piano | Reading from a score
test: Which sentence shows someone improvising? | The campers waited quietly for the bus home | *The campers tied a sheet between two trees to make a shelter when the rain started | The campers read the map and followed the marked path | The campers ate their lunch at the café
test: Nadia says improvise means to copy a plan exactly. Is she right? | No, it means to write a plan first | Yes, it means to stop and wait | Yes, it means to follow the plan carefully | *No, it means doing something without a plan
exam: Which word best fits? «The drummer had no music, so she ___ a rhythm that the whole band loved.» | cancelled | copied | forgot | *improvised
